  "notes": "San Diego retro-soul contralto — \"jazz-and-Winehouse-lineage phrasing over Warner-scale orchestral production\". The kit is the retro-soul one the entry names: \"damped snare hard on 2&4, tambourine doubling, march-style triplet fills, no 16th hat carpet, and a full stop-time break before the final chorus\", plus \"programmed hybrid kits on the up-tempos\" — so the snare carries a rimshot layer over a small displacement, the hat is a non-continuous 8th at 0.14, the tambourine mirrors the clap, `snareRoll.subdivisions` include both triplet forms for the march fills, and `fillBeforeSection` covers the stop-time. The band is the entry's 62–104. Harmony is \"`i–iv–VII–III`, ii–V turnarounds, diminished approach chords, and a whole-step lift for the last chorus\", so both families are the entry's own and `susOrDimProb` carries the approach chords. The melodic claim is a dynamic arc: \"deep contralto with heavy behind-the-beat placement, wide pitch bends, growl ornament, and an extreme quiet-to-belt dynamic arc across the song — note-density low in the verse and very high in the final chorus\". A single model cannot ramp by section, so the density range runs 2–8 with `endVariation` and a raised `halfStepDissonance` for the growl and bends, and the arrangement gives the key-lifted final chorus its own weight. Bass is \"upright or flatwound electric bass, short and round, walking under the changes\". ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum number and all melody figures; the research marks the tempo and drum values as inference. ⚠ `session.keys` is INFERRED too: the entry's Keys line names no key at all and ends only \"Flat keys dominate.\", so the five values are this author's flat-side reading of that single constraint rather than a list the research gives. ⚠ The large half-time subset the entry describes (snare on 3) is marked [LOW-CONF] there and is not encoded here.",
